On the basis of formulations previously established for analogous gas–solid reactions, the behavior of alkalis in the blast furnace process was incorporated into the six-phase CFD framework. The adopted approach accounts for the heterogeneous nature of the reactions, in which the interaction between the gas phase and the particle surface plays a decisive role in the kinetics of alkali transformation. In particular, the model formulation accounts for both the availability of reaction sites on the burden particles and transport phenomena at the gas–solid interface, which govern the release, recirculation, and potential condensation of alkalis under varying thermal and chemical conditions within the furnace. This representation ensures that the dynamic contribution of alkalis to the overall furnace operation is consistently captured within the numerical framework. Detailed chemical reactions newly introduced in this study to account for the alkali cycle and incorporation into the solid phase are shown in Table  3 .
